{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For index laws: identify the rule, apply to simplify the expression",
   "For surds: find the largest square factor, then simplify √(a×b) = √a × √b",
   "To rationalise: multiply numerator and denominator by the surd (or conjugate)",
   "For bounds: lower = value − half unit, upper = value + half unit"
  ],
  "title": "How to Use Index Laws, Surds and Bounds",
  "content": "<p><strong>Index laws:</strong> \\(a^m \\times a^n = a^{m+n}\\), \\(a^m \\div a^n = a^{m-n}\\), \\((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\\), \\(a^{-n} = \\frac{1}{a^n}\\), \\(a^{1/n} = \\sqrt[n]{a}\\), \\(a^{m/n} = (\\sqrt[n]{a})^m\\).</p><p><strong>Surds</strong> are irrational roots left in exact form, e.g. \\(\\sqrt{2}\\). Key rules: \\(\\sqrt{a} \\times \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}\\), \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{a}}{\\sqrt{b}} = \\sqrt{a/b}\\). Simplify by finding the largest square factor.</p><p><strong>Rationalising the denominator:</strong> Multiply top and bottom by the surd. For \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}\\), multiply by \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{a}}{\\sqrt{a}}\\). For \\(\\frac{1}{a + \\sqrt{b}}\\), multiply by the conjugate \\(a - \\sqrt{b}\\).</p><p><strong>Bounds:</strong> When a measurement is rounded, the true value lies within an <em>error interval</em>. For a length of 5.3 cm (to 1 d.p.): lower bound = 5.25, upper bound = 5.35. Use bounds to find maximum/minimum values of calculations.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Simplify \\(\\sqrt{72}\\)</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Find largest square factor: \\(72 = 36 \\times 2\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(\\sqrt{72} = \\sqrt{36 \\times 2} = \\sqrt{36} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 6\\sqrt{2}\\)</p>"
 },
